prompt:
Why do all my buttons alert 3?

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = () => alert(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

text:
`var` gives `i` function scope, so the whole loop shares one `i`. The click handlers don't run until after the loop ends, and by then `i` is 3. Each handler reads that same variable, so each one alerts 3.

The fix is to declare `i` with `let`, which creates a new `i` for each pass through the loop:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = () => alert(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

Now each handler keeps the `i` from its own iteration, so the buttons alert 0, 1 and 2.

If you have to keep `var`, copy the value into a variable that belongs to each iteration:

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const n = i;
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = () => alert(n);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

Here `const n` is block-scoped, so each pass gets its own `n`.
